    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d150">

                <pb facs="0347" n="273" sameAs="frus:frus1941-43/pg273"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Hopkins Papers</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The British Minister of Supply</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Beaverbrook</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the President’s Special Assistant</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Hopkins</hi>)<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d150-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, [<date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-12-26">December 26,
                        1941</date>.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>You should work on Churchill. He is being advised. He is open minded &amp; needs
                    discussion.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d150-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d150-f1-content">
                <p>A memorandum by Hopkins, dated January 3, 1942, attached to this file, reads as
                    follows: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“This is a note that Lord Beaverbrook handed me when the Unity of Command
                            was being discussed and Churchill seemed to be a stumbling block.</p>
                        <p>“It was as a result of getting this message that I arranged that Marshall
                            see Churchill.”</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <p>For the discussion of December 26 to which this note pertains, see <hi
                        rendition="#italic">ante</hi>, <ref target="#pg103">p. 103</ref>. For the
                    meeting of Marshall and Churchill on the morning of December 27, see <hi
                        rendition="#italic">ante</hi>, <ref target="#pg108">p. 108</ref>.</p>
                <p>A facsimile reproduction of the Beaverbrook note appears in Sherwood, p. 456.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
